depends: re-verify a cached source archive before reusing it.

Patch 0009 keeps simplybs' download cache across targets, which is what
makes this build finish in one day rather than several and what keeps the
hand-staged Xcode xip alive. It also makes one failure mode permanent.
DownloadSource only downloads when the file is absent:

	if _, err := os.Stat(sourcePath); os.IsNotExist(err) {

and downloadWithResume deletes a file only after a *completed* transfer
fails its sha256. A run killed mid-transfer -- Ctrl-C out of
build-darwin.sh, an OOM kill, an interrupted docker build -- therefore
leaves a truncated archive that is never re-fetched and never re-checked.
Before 0009 the next sbs-cleanup cleared it; now nothing does. Every later
run then dies in the extract step on a corrupt archive, and the recovery is
a manual rm of a cache path the user has to work out.

Hash a cached archive against the sha256 its package already pins and
re-download it if it does not match. DownloadFile resumes from what is on
disk, so the common case is a range request for the missing tail rather
than the whole file, and a completed transfer that still mismatches is
deleted by the check already in downloadWithResume.

Only the tar kinds are checked. "git" is a clone bundle whose Sha256 means
something else to ExtractGitCloneBundle, and "blob" is the Apple xip: that
one is staged into the cache by hand rather than downloaded, its mismatch
is deliberately overridable (XCODE_XIP_ALLOW_MISMATCH), and build-darwin.sh
and build-ios.sh already re-stage it when the cached size differs.

The cost is a sha256 pass over the cached archives at the start of each
target. Against targets that each spend hours compiling a cross toolchain
from source, reading a few GB off local disk does not show up.

